<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">French First Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French First Republic was proclaimed in September 1792 after the overthrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Its armies fought the European monarchies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> and annexed the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and small enclaves such as Salm, abolishing ecclesiastical principalities and imperial cities there. It ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> proclaimed himself emperor in 1804.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-first-republic.jpg" alt="French First Republic" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>French First Republic</b> about 17,000 people were sentenced to death during the Terror of 1793–1794 before Napoleon took power; the Second Republic ended in a coup by its own president, Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte, in 1851, and the Weimar Republic in Hitler’s dictatorship.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>On its first full day, the National Convention, created after the insurrection of 10 August 1792, proclaimed the abolition of the French monarchy, giving rise to the <b>French First Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Revolutionary France</b> occupied the city in 1792 and annexed it in 1797.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Free Imperial City of Speyer</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Revolutionary France</b> annexed it in 1797.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-worms">Free Imperial City of Worms</a></li><li><blockquote>Surrounded by <b>Revolutionary France</b> and cut off by its blockade, the inhabitants voted to join France, and the French First Republic annexed the principality in 1793.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-salm">Principality of Salm</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Revolutionary France</b> annexed the principality in 1795 and dissolved the abbeys.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-stavelot-malmedy">Principality of Stavelot-Malmedy</a></li></ul></section>
